Identify the correspondent for this document based on its content.

A correspondent is the sender (if you received the document) or the recipient (if you sent it). It can be:
- a company or organization (e.g. "Kaufland", "Deutsche Telekom", "TransferGo")
- a person (use first + last name only — no titles like "Dr.", "Mr.", "Frau")
- a government agency or authority (e.g. "Bezirksamt Pankow", "Bundesamt für Migration und Flüchtlinge")
- an email address (only if no real entity name appears in the text)

CORE PRINCIPLE — read this carefully:

The correspondent MUST be an entity whose name LITERALLY APPEARS as text in <content>. Typical places where the name lands in OCR text:
- the first few lines (where letterhead, store name, or page header usually print)
- "From:" / "Absender:" / "Sender:" fields
- return address blocks
- signature lines near the bottom
- any text captured from a stamp, seal, or footer

If the entity exists only visually in the original (a logo, an unreadable stamp) and its name is NOT present anywhere in <content>, you CANNOT identify it. In that case, output exactly:
Unknown

The <example_correspondents> list is provided ONLY as a normalization reference for capitalization and spelling. It is NOT a menu of allowed answers. You MUST NEVER pick a name from this list if the text in <content> does not reference that entity.

PROCEDURE — follow in order:

Step 1. Read <content> and look for entity names. Pay particular attention to the first few lines and to any explicit "From / Sender / Absender" markers.

Step 2. If you found an entity name in Step 1:
   a. If it matches an entry in <example_correspondents> (case- and spelling-tolerant, ignoring minor format differences), output that entry EXACTLY as it appears in the list.
   b. Otherwise, output the entity as written in <content>. Preserve original spelling and language. For instance, do NOT translate "Bundesamt für Migration und Flüchtlinge" into {{.Language}}.

Step 3. If <content> does NOT contain any clearly named entity — for example a receipt whose store name didn't OCR, or a stamped notice whose stamp is unreadable — output exactly:
Unknown

ADDITIONAL RULES:

- Never pick a name from <example_correspondents> if the text in <content> does not reference that entity. When in doubt → Unknown.
- Never combine multiple parties. Pick the single most relevant entity (usually the sender on incoming documents, the recipient on outgoing).
- Never invent, never guess. If you are uncertain between several candidates, output Unknown.
- For companies: drop legal suffixes (GmbH, AG, Ltd, Inc., LLC, S.a.r.l., UAB) ONLY IF the matched <example_correspondents> entry uses the shortened form. Otherwise keep the name as written in <content>.
- For persons: first + last name only. Drop titles.
- Never output a name appearing in <blacklisted_correspondents>.

EXAMPLES OF CORRECT BEHAVIOR:

- <content> starts with "Kaufland\nStorkower Straße..." → `Kaufland`
- Meldebestätigung whose <content> shows the form template but the agency/stamp line did not OCR → `Unknown`  (do NOT pick a random correspondent from the list)
- Document whose only entity names in <content> are the user's own (recipient on an outgoing letter, with no other party named) → `Unknown`
- <content> shows "Microsoft Ireland Operations Limited" while the list contains "Microsoft" → `Microsoft`
- <content> shows "Bundesamt für Migration und Flüchtlinge" with no matching list entry → `Bundesamt für Migration und Flüchtlinge`

OUTPUT: exactly one line — either a correspondent name produced by the procedure above, or the literal word `Unknown`. No quotes, no commentary, no explanation.

<example_correspondents>
{{.AvailableCorrespondents | join ", "}}
</example_correspondents>

<blacklisted_correspondents>
{{.BlackList | join ", "}}
</blacklisted_correspondents>

<title>{{.Title}}</title>
<content>
{{.Content}}
</content>
